proof: aaru | opensuse | vendor-cap
date: 2026-07-15
lane/VM: test-openSUSE (Leap 16.0, autologin Plasma X11 -> seat0), OBS repo
user groups: "gmipf wheel" (NO cdrom/disk/plugdev) -> groupless
drive: PLEXTOR PX-760A (USB) -> /dev/sr1
disc: MIXED-MODE CD (track 1 data, track 2 STEREO AUDIO) -- audio track needed so
      aaru takes its Plextor D8 disc-offset-detection path (a plain data CD uses only
      whitelisted 0xBE and does not exercise the cap).
mechanism: openSUSE permissions framework (permissions.d/aaru + permctl)
Note: aaru first-run GDPR wizard skipped by seeding ~/.config/Aaru.json GdprCompliance=1.

--- delivery ---
$ /usr/sbin/getcap /usr/lib64/aaru/aaru -> cap_sys_rawio=ep
$ rpm -qf /usr/lib64/aaru/aaru -> aaru-6.0.0~alpha.19-lp160.31.1.x86_64

--- A/B (disc-offset detection via Plextor D8) ---
GREEN (cap):     aaru media dump /dev/sr1 ~/ag.aaruf -f -p 0
                 -> "Disc offset is 5044 bytes (1261 samples)"   # D8 vendor read OK
RED (setcap -r): identical command
                 -> "Disc write offset is unknown, dump may not be correct."  # D8 blocked
restore:         setcap cap_sys_rawio=ep -> getcap shows cap again

MECHANISM: identical operation was strace-confirmed on fedora -- the offset read is a
SG_IO with CDB 0xD8 (Plextor, >=0xC0) that returns EPERM without CAP_SYS_RAWIO
(see proofs/aaru-fedora-vendor-cap.log). Same kernel gate applies here.

VERDICT: PROVEN. permctl-delivered cap on aaru is load-bearing for the Plextor D8
disc-offset read; without it aaru cannot determine the offset.
